WEBVTT

00:00.120 --> 00:01.650
Instructor: All right, so I wanna demonstrate

00:01.650 --> 00:05.580
how we could use this -i option, the interactive rebase,

00:05.580 --> 00:09.690
in order to clean up a messy branch, a messy set of commits.

00:09.690 --> 00:14.130
So I have a messy set of commits that you can try this on.

00:14.130 --> 00:16.440
The link will be included with this video

00:16.440 --> 00:19.080
as a resource, interactive-rebase-demo.

00:19.080 --> 00:20.730
It's not very creative.

00:20.730 --> 00:22.440
But we have a bunch of commits in here.

00:22.440 --> 00:25.710
Well, not a huge amount, but what, 8 or 10 commits?

00:25.710 --> 00:28.590
And there are some missteps here.

00:28.590 --> 00:32.580
For example, I started with initial commit. That's fine.

00:32.580 --> 00:35.430
But most of my commits are using the imperative.

00:35.430 --> 00:36.750
What is it, imperative voice?

00:36.750 --> 00:38.460
Imperative, it's not tense, is it?

00:38.460 --> 00:40.380
Present tense, imperative voice?

00:40.380 --> 00:44.880
Whatever the grammatical term is, right, fix x, add x.

00:44.880 --> 00:48.510
But then past tense, "I added project files."

00:48.510 --> 00:51.990
Okay, so I wanna rename that or rework that commit message

00:51.990 --> 00:54.000
before I share that with somebody.

00:54.000 --> 00:56.970
And this is a silly example, but often, you know,

00:56.970 --> 01:00.120
you might be doing some work locally on some project

01:00.120 --> 01:01.380
where you're just working on your own.

01:01.380 --> 01:03.210
You're not paying attention to commit guidelines

01:03.210 --> 01:05.610
and messaging guidelines for the commit messages.

01:05.610 --> 01:07.410
But then as you get ready to share this branch

01:07.410 --> 01:10.410
with people and with, you know, let's say, somebody

01:10.410 --> 01:13.230
who maintains a big open-source project or your boss,

01:13.230 --> 01:14.250
you might want to go through

01:14.250 --> 01:17.670
and rename commits to conform to some pattern

01:17.670 --> 01:20.100
that the project is asking you to do.

01:20.100 --> 01:23.670
Anyway, I also have a situation here.

01:23.670 --> 01:26.640
I added some Bootstrap files in, and then I committed,

01:26.640 --> 01:28.290
and then I realized, oh, whoops,

01:28.290 --> 01:30.030
forgot to add Bootstrap JS script.

01:30.030 --> 01:32.640
I forgot to add the JavaScript portion.

01:32.640 --> 01:34.950
So I wanna go through, and I wanna combine that, actually,

01:34.950 --> 01:37.440
into one commit, because they're doing the same thing.

01:37.440 --> 01:40.560
It's part of adding Bootstrap into my file.

01:40.560 --> 01:42.360
If you don't know Bootstrap, it's okay.

01:42.360 --> 01:45.300
Basically, I started a task, and I didn't quite finish it.

01:45.300 --> 01:47.790
So I have a second commit that also has to do

01:47.790 --> 01:49.560
with this same topic.

01:49.560 --> 01:52.920
Then I have this, I added a top nav bar to this website,

01:52.920 --> 01:55.410
and then I made a commit, but then I found some typos.

01:55.410 --> 01:56.250
So I made a commit.

01:56.250 --> 01:58.380
And then, oh no, I found more typos.

01:58.380 --> 01:59.820
So I made another commit.

01:59.820 --> 02:02.100
I don't wanna share that with people.

02:02.100 --> 02:03.630
I want those typos to be fixed,

02:03.630 --> 02:05.340
but I wanna collapse those down

02:05.340 --> 02:07.530
and just merge 'em in with this commit.

02:07.530 --> 02:09.307
And then, also, I've got this last commit,

02:09.307 --> 02:11.130
"my cat made this commit."

02:11.130 --> 02:14.190
I wanna just completely drop that. I don't want that.

02:14.190 --> 02:16.350
All right, so I just wanna get rid of it.

02:16.350 --> 02:18.210
So these are all things that we can do

02:18.210 --> 02:20.370
using interactive rebase.

02:20.370 --> 02:22.740
So I'm gonna start by cloning this project.

02:22.740 --> 02:26.790
So let's copy this URL, and then I'll clone it down.

02:26.790 --> 02:31.143
So make sure I'm not in a repo, git clone, this URL.

02:32.640 --> 02:37.110
Okay, so if we take a look, cd into there,

02:37.110 --> 02:40.170
first thing I'll do is run git log oneline.

02:40.170 --> 02:43.650
You can see that history, right, initial commit,

02:43.650 --> 02:47.190
this one in the wrong tense, "I added project files,"

02:47.190 --> 02:48.840
the Bootstrap stuff.

02:48.840 --> 02:51.120
The only thing that has changed, actually,

02:51.120 --> 02:55.320
is that I created a README on GitHub, I forgot about that,

02:55.320 --> 02:59.550
and I did not include that commit in this commit log.

02:59.550 --> 03:02.430
So there's another commit that was just added at the top,

03:02.430 --> 03:03.570
create the README.

03:03.570 --> 03:07.740
Anyway, so what I'm gonna do, I'm gonna make a branch first.

03:07.740 --> 03:09.300
So this is just on the main branch,

03:09.300 --> 03:11.310
but imagine we're working on some feature.

03:11.310 --> 03:13.983
So I'm gonna make a branch, git switch -c,

03:14.970 --> 03:17.820
my feature, my-feat.

03:17.820 --> 03:19.650
All right, so I'm on this branch,

03:19.650 --> 03:22.500
and I am ready to share this code with people,

03:22.500 --> 03:25.890
but then I realize this is actually pretty terrible.

03:25.890 --> 03:27.870
I wanna go through and clean up some of these commits,

03:27.870 --> 03:30.960
condense them, maybe drop some, rename them

03:30.960 --> 03:32.670
before anyone sees them.

03:32.670 --> 03:34.470
So as we talked about,

03:34.470 --> 03:38.190
we can use this git rebase command with the -i option,

03:38.190 --> 03:40.800
and then specify how far back we wanna go.

03:40.800 --> 03:45.150
And, again, I'm not specifying a branch to rebase onto.

03:45.150 --> 03:47.190
I just wanna rebase in place

03:47.190 --> 03:49.230
on top of the branch we're currently in.

03:49.230 --> 03:53.820
In other words, redo some number of commits on this branch.

03:53.820 --> 03:55.650
So I need to figure out how many.

03:55.650 --> 03:57.210
I'll just go, ah, I mean,

03:57.210 --> 03:59.010
I'm not gonna mess with initial commit,

03:59.010 --> 04:01.950
so I could just do 1, 2, 3, 4,

04:01.950 --> 04:04.590
5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

04:04.590 --> 04:08.460
I could go back nine commits and rebase that, all right?

04:08.460 --> 04:12.360
So before I do anything, I'm gonna take a screenshot of this

04:12.360 --> 04:14.430
just so that I can show you afterwards.

04:14.430 --> 04:16.950
All right, so now I'm going to run the command.

04:16.950 --> 04:20.970
So it's git rebase -i, for interactive.

04:20.970 --> 04:23.580
And then I have to specify how many commits back

04:23.580 --> 04:25.320
I want to recreate.

04:25.320 --> 04:27.390
So if I only wanted to edit something,

04:27.390 --> 04:29.100
you know, five commits ago,

04:29.100 --> 04:32.340
there's no reason to go back 50 commits.

04:32.340 --> 04:34.410
Generally, right, we wanna minimize the amount

04:34.410 --> 04:36.930
of rewriting history we do.

04:36.930 --> 04:40.140
So in our case, though, looking at this log,

04:40.140 --> 04:42.000
I've screenshotted it here,

04:42.000 --> 04:44.340
I wanna go back and at least to this one,

04:44.340 --> 04:46.350
as we talked about, so that's nine,

04:46.350 --> 04:47.757
so that's exactly what I'm gonna do,

04:47.757 --> 04:51.480
git rebase -i, and then HEAD~9,

04:51.480 --> 04:52.950
nine commits ago.

04:52.950 --> 04:56.100
Also remember that I am not specifying a branch.

04:56.100 --> 04:59.910
We are not rebasing onto a branch. We're rebasing in place.

04:59.910 --> 05:02.400
So that's why I didn't say, like, git rebase master

05:02.400 --> 05:03.660
or anything like that.

05:03.660 --> 05:06.960
Okay, so when I hit Enter here, something weird will happen.

05:06.960 --> 05:09.030
It will open up our text editor,

05:09.030 --> 05:11.280
and it's not the most interactive thing ever,

05:11.280 --> 05:12.990
but it opens up our text editor,

05:12.990 --> 05:15.240
and it will give us a list of our commits

05:15.240 --> 05:16.950
with these commands next to them.

05:16.950 --> 05:19.560
It will say pick by default.

05:19.560 --> 05:22.680
But then I have a bunch of options I can instead use.

05:22.680 --> 05:24.720
So I'm gonna sort of take my time

05:24.720 --> 05:26.910
showing those options to you in the next video or two.

05:26.910 --> 05:29.160
But in this video, we're gonna focus on one

05:29.160 --> 05:31.020
which is called reword.

05:31.020 --> 05:32.610
But first I've gotta run this.

05:32.610 --> 05:35.910
So I hit Enter, and it opens my text editor.

05:35.910 --> 05:37.380
make this a bit larger.

05:37.380 --> 05:39.180
It shows me all the commits

05:39.180 --> 05:42.180
not ever in my repository right,

05:42.180 --> 05:43.650
only the ones that I selected,

05:43.650 --> 05:46.470
so nine, going back nine commits.

05:46.470 --> 05:49.170
So we don't see initial commit.

05:49.170 --> 05:50.940
That was 10 commits ago.

05:50.940 --> 05:55.140
It stops with "I added project files."

05:55.140 --> 05:57.810
Also notice the order is reversed, right.

05:57.810 --> 05:59.340
Normally, with git log,

05:59.340 --> 06:01.710
we see the oldest commit at the bottom

06:01.710 --> 06:03.210
and the newest commit up top.

06:03.210 --> 06:06.870
Here, it's reversed, oldest, newest.

06:06.870 --> 06:10.440
But this is because basically this is a list of commands

06:10.440 --> 06:13.440
that we're telling Git to do for each commit,

06:13.440 --> 06:15.840
starting with this one, do this.

06:15.840 --> 06:17.520
We'll talk about pick in a moment.

06:17.520 --> 06:19.020
But if I change what these are,

06:19.020 --> 06:21.690
if I say drop something, rename, edit,

06:21.690 --> 06:23.160
it's gonna go through an order

06:23.160 --> 06:26.100
and take this commit and follow this instruction.

06:26.100 --> 06:27.840
And then this next one,

06:27.840 --> 06:29.970
take this commit, follow that instruction.

06:29.970 --> 06:32.700
So if I change those instructions, it goes through an order,

06:32.700 --> 06:35.820
and it does whatever I say to these commits.

06:35.820 --> 06:37.500
So it starts at the beginning, right,

06:37.500 --> 06:38.880
not the beginning of our project,

06:38.880 --> 06:40.350
not the beginning of the branch,

06:40.350 --> 06:43.260
but the beginning of this subsection of commits,

06:43.260 --> 06:45.030
nine commits, it starts at this one,

06:45.030 --> 06:48.990
and then reconstructs going towards the most recent commit.

06:48.990 --> 06:52.050
So there's a list of these commands that we have available

06:52.050 --> 06:53.580
down at the bottom here,

06:53.580 --> 06:56.610
quite a few, pick, reword, edit, squash, fixup,

06:56.610 --> 06:59.160
exec, break, drop, label, reset, merge.

06:59.160 --> 07:02.070
I've highlighted some of the more important ones here.

07:02.070 --> 07:05.850
The first is the default one that it's already using, pick.

07:05.850 --> 07:09.570
It's just a way of saying, yes, use this commit.

07:09.570 --> 07:12.720
Keep it, don't change it, just use this commit, pick it.

07:12.720 --> 07:15.600
But in this video, I'll also show you reword,

07:15.600 --> 07:18.150
which is how I can reword the commit message.

07:18.150 --> 07:19.770
So I'll edit the commit message.

07:19.770 --> 07:22.410
We'll still keep that commit, right, in history.

07:22.410 --> 07:25.230
It's not gonna go away or anything, the contents of it.

07:25.230 --> 07:26.550
But I want a new message.

07:26.550 --> 07:29.467
So in our example, I have this one in past tense,

07:29.467 --> 07:31.830
"I added project files."

07:31.830 --> 07:33.990
I want to reword that.

07:33.990 --> 07:36.330
So I don't just edit it here in place.

07:36.330 --> 07:40.710
What I'm gonna do is reword. That's the command I want.

07:40.710 --> 07:43.410
I could also just do r, but I'm gonna do reword,

07:43.410 --> 07:44.850
just to make it clearer for you.

07:44.850 --> 07:48.360
I'm gonna save this, and this whole time, right,

07:48.360 --> 07:50.280
my editor has just been waiting,

07:50.280 --> 07:53.010
tapping its toe, just waiting for me to finish.

07:53.010 --> 07:56.043
So I'm gonna close out of here now and close this window.

07:57.330 --> 08:01.260
That was the only command I wanted to do, the only change.

08:01.260 --> 08:02.970
And now it opens up something new.

08:02.970 --> 08:05.460
It says, "Waiting for your editor to close," again.

08:05.460 --> 08:08.520
But this time it opened up that commit message,

08:08.520 --> 08:10.380
and it's gonna allow me to change this.

08:10.380 --> 08:12.660
Instead of saying, "I added project files,"

08:12.660 --> 08:17.070
I'll just say, "add project files," okay?

08:17.070 --> 08:20.670
And then I'll save, and then I'm gonna close out of here.

08:20.670 --> 08:23.970
Also, you can see interactive rebase in progress.

08:23.970 --> 08:25.890
It knows, right, that we're in the middle of this.

08:25.890 --> 08:28.530
It's actually the very first commit that it's rebasing

08:28.530 --> 08:29.850
is the one I reworded.

08:29.850 --> 08:31.680
Everything else is just being picked,

08:31.680 --> 08:33.690
meaning keep it, don't reword it,

08:33.690 --> 08:35.880
don't do anything, just keep it.

08:35.880 --> 08:39.270
So I close out, and I'll full-screen this.

08:39.270 --> 08:41.550
Here we are, waiting for your editor to close,

08:41.550 --> 08:42.420
okay, it did.

08:42.420 --> 08:45.720
And then it says, "Successfully rebased and updated."

08:45.720 --> 08:49.350
Okay, so I type git status. Nothing has changed.

08:49.350 --> 08:52.443
However, if I type git log, and I do oneline,

08:53.340 --> 08:55.710
the first thing that we'll notice, hopefully,

08:55.710 --> 08:59.250
is this new commit message, "add project files,"

08:59.250 --> 09:03.120
instead of "added," or "I added," I think is what it said.

09:03.120 --> 09:06.930
So we changed that message. We reworded that message.

09:06.930 --> 09:08.980
So I'm gonna screenshot this and compare.

09:09.930 --> 09:12.487
Okay, so this is what we had before,

09:12.487 --> 09:14.400
"I added project files."

09:14.400 --> 09:15.720
That was our second commit.

09:15.720 --> 09:18.750
Now our second commit is "add project files."

09:18.750 --> 09:22.200
But more importantly, or just as important,

09:22.200 --> 09:26.850
by changing that commit, by renaming it or rewording it,

09:26.850 --> 09:28.770
that changes the commit hash.

09:28.770 --> 09:31.560
I'll compare them as best I can here.

09:31.560 --> 09:34.170
Let's see, maybe zoom these in a bit.

09:34.170 --> 09:36.180
Well, it's not a perfect comparison.

09:36.180 --> 09:37.890
The screenshots are different sizes.

09:37.890 --> 09:40.260
But you can see that first commit, initial commit,

09:40.260 --> 09:42.960
is unchanged, but then every other commit

09:42.960 --> 09:45.330
afterwards actually was rewritten.

09:45.330 --> 09:46.830
It has a new hash.

09:46.830 --> 09:50.490
Because this one changed, we changed that message,

09:50.490 --> 09:51.930
the whole commit changes,

09:51.930 --> 09:54.240
and every subsequent commit has to change

09:54.240 --> 09:56.400
because every commit takes into account

09:56.400 --> 09:58.650
its parent commit, it's rehashed.

09:58.650 --> 10:01.380
Long story short, all new commits here,

10:01.380 --> 10:02.580
but that's all that's changed.

10:02.580 --> 10:05.610
We renamed or reworded one commit message

10:05.610 --> 10:08.880
that made every other commit afterwards change.

10:08.880 --> 10:11.820
The commit hash is regenerated, it's a new commit,

10:11.820 --> 10:14.700
but the messages are the same, the contents are the same.

10:14.700 --> 10:17.310
We didn't rebase onto another branch, right,

10:17.310 --> 10:19.350
so our branch still looks the same

10:19.350 --> 10:21.270
and same number of commits.

10:21.270 --> 10:23.430
It's just that we used this one command so far,

10:23.430 --> 10:26.730
the reword command, to change the commit message.

10:26.730 --> 10:28.500
So in the next video, I'm gonna show you

10:28.500 --> 10:30.840
how we can actually combine commits

10:30.840 --> 10:33.120
so that I can get rid of some of these redundant things,

10:33.120 --> 10:36.660
like combine the two bootstrap commits here

10:36.660 --> 10:40.230
or the two bug fix commits up here with the nav bar.

10:40.230 --> 10:41.910
So that's what I'll show you in the next video.

10:41.910 --> 10:44.760
We still use interactive rebase. It's just a new command.
